<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Central African Federation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland, or Central African Federation, joined the British territories of Southern Rhodesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> and Nyasaland from 1953 to 1963. Dominated by white settlers and governed from Salisbury, it faced strong African opposition. It was dissolved in 1963, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> became independent and Southern Rhodesia declared independence as Rhodesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland.jpg" alt="Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, vector version by Lupin, Greentubing, Fenn-O-maniC and NikNaks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Rhodesia_and_Nyasaland_(1953%E2%80%931963).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1929 the Hilton Young Commission concluded that the interests of Nyasaland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> lay with each other and with the self-governing colony of Southern Rhodesia. In 1938 the Bledisloe Commission stopped short of recommending federation and advised an inter-territorial council instead; the Central African Council, with consultative powers only, was set up in 1945.</p><p>In 1950 the British government decided to examine closer union again, and conferences followed in 1951. They were arduous, because Southern Rhodesia and the northern territories had very different traditions in their treatment of black Africans. Sir Andrew Cohen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> was one of the central architects of the agreement. The Southern Rhodesian prime minister, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-huggins">Godfrey Huggins</a>, preferred a unitary state, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> opposed it because Southern Rhodesia's large European population would then dominate the franchise. In a referendum on 9 April 1953 a little more than 25,000 white Southern Rhodesians voted for federation and nearly 15,000 against; a majority of black Africans in all three territories were resolutely against it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Federation was established on 1 August 1953 with a Governor-General as the Queen's representative. It had a unicameral Federal Assembly, which included an African Affairs Board to safeguard African interests, and a Supreme Court inaugurated in 1955. Huggins, created Viscount Malvern in 1955, was prime minister until 1956 and was followed by Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roy-welensky">Roy Welensky</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>. The Federation's economy grew, and the Kariba hydro-electric station was announced in 1955, but the average income of a European remained about ten times that of an African in the cash economy. The European population of Southern Rhodesia rose from 135,000 before federation to 223,000 by its end.</p><p>African opposition grew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a> returned to Nyasaland in July 1958 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a> became leader of the Zambian African National Congress in October; the Congress was banned in March 1959 and Kaunda imprisoned for nine months. After unrest in Nyasaland in early 1959 the authorities declared a state of emergency and arrested Banda and other leaders. A commission led by Viscount Monckton was appointed to advise on the future of the Federation, and by 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the federal government had agreed that Nyasaland might secede; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> was given the same status a year later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Victoria Falls Conference met in 1963, partly to save the Federation and partly to dissolve it, which was done formally on 31 December 1963. Its assets went to the territorial governments, and Southern Rhodesia received the great majority of them, including those of the federal army. Nyasaland became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> in July 1964 under Banda, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> in October under Kaunda. On 11 November 1965 the government of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a> proclaimed Southern Rhodesia's unilateral independence from the United Kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</b>, created in 1953 after the Colonial Secretary Oliver Lyttelton dropped the condition of sounding out African opinion, was intensely unpopular with the African majority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From August 1953 to the end of December 1963 it belonged to the <b>Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Federations created by colonial powers, such as the West Indies Federation and the <b>Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</b>, failed within a few years, and in the 19th century Gran Colombia and the Federal Republic of Central America had dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</b>, which Banda had opposed, was dissolved in 1963.</blockquote><a href="/state/malawi">Malawi</a></li><li><blockquote>Northern Rhodesia became the Republic of Zambia on 24 October 1964, after the <b>Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</b> was dissolved on 31 December 1963.</blockquote><a href="/state/zambia">Zambia</a></li></ul></section>
